[{"figure_index": 1, "page_no": 4, "caption": "Figure 1: BFT enhances the outputs of DeepSeek-R1-Distill series (14B, 32B and 70B). a : In the medical domain, theme-wise evaluation. b : In the medical domain, axis-wise evaluation. c : Forgetting evaluation in the general domain. We evaluated the general capabilities of LLMs previously fine-tuned on the OpenAI Health Bench Consensus subset using the MMLU benchmark. d : Forgetting evaluation in the general domain. We evaluated the general capabilities of LLMs previously fine-tuned on the OpenAI Health Bench Consensus subset using the CMMLU benchmark. e : In the biology domain, we applied BFT (blue) and SFT (orange) to fine-tune DeepSeek-R1-Distill series (14B, 32B and 70B). We evaluated LLMs on three biological process reasoning benchmarks. We evaluate ROUGE scores (recall-oriented understudy for gisting evaluation) between the generated final pathway names and ground truths, specifically ROUGE-L (longest common subsequence), ROUGE-1 (1-gram) and ROUGE-2 (2-gram) scores. f : We compared the BFT-based DeepSeek-R1-Distill (70B) against the latest baselines for biological process reasoning.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure composed of bar charts comparing model performance across tasks, model sizes, datasets, and methods.\n\nVisible panels and labels:\n- Panel **a**: Three grouped bar charts titled **Theme-wise (70B)**, **Theme-wise (32B)**, and **Theme-wise (14B)**. Y-axis is **Score**. X-axis compares **BFT** and **SFT**. Legend includes themes: `emergency_referrals`, `communication`, `hedging`, `complex_responses`, `health_data_tasks`, `global_health`, and `context_seeking`.\n- Panel **b**: Three grouped bar charts titled **Axis-wise (70B)**, **Axis-wise (32B)**, and **Axis-wise (14B)**. Y-axis is **Score**. X-axis compares **BFT** and **SFT**. Legend includes axes: `communication_quality`, `instruction_following`, `accuracy`, `context_awareness`, and `completeness`.\n- Panel **c**: Three grouped bar charts titled **MMLU (70B)**, **MMLU (32B)**, and **MMLU (14B)**. Y-axis is **Accuracy**. Categories include **STEM**, **Sociology**, **Humanities**, and **Other**. Legend compares **Base**, **BFT**, and **SFT**.\n- Panel **d**: Three grouped bar charts titled **CMMLU (70B)**, **CMMLU (32B)**, and **CMMLU (14B)**. Y-axis is **Accuracy**. Categories include **STEM**, **Sociology**, **Humanities**, and **Other**. Legend compares **Base**, **BFT**, and **SFT**.\n- Panel **e**: Nine grouped bar charts for summarization-style metrics across model sizes **14B**, **32B**, and **70B**. Metrics are **Rouge-L**, **Rouge-1**, and **Rouge-2**. X-axis datasets include **MsigDB**, **NeST**, and **Gene Ontology**. Legend compares **BFT** and **SFT**.\n- Panel **f**: Three grouped bar charts titled **MsigDB**, **NeST**, and **Gene Ontology**. Metrics on the x-axis are **Rouge-L**, **Rouge-1**, and **Rouge-2**. Legend compares methods **BFT**, **GeneAgent**, and **GPT-Hu**. Error bars are shown.\n\nBiological source objects:\n- The figure explicitly references biological or biomedical knowledge sources/datasets: **MSigDB**, **NeST**, and **Gene Ontology**.\n- No microscopy images, organisms, cells, tissues, molecules, or anatomical structures are visually shown.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- The figure compares model variants and training/finetuning conditions labeled **Base**, **BFT**, and **SFT** across model sizes **14B**, **32B**, and **70B**.\n- It presents quantitative evaluation outputs as grouped bar plots with accuracy, score, and ROUGE metrics.\n\nFindings visible from the figure:\n- Theme-wise and axis-wise scores vary substantially by model size and training condition.\n- MMLU and CMMLU accuracy are compared across subject groups for Base, BFT, and SFT.\n- ROUGE-based performance is shown for biomedical/gene-related datasets, with BFT and SFT compared in panel e and BFT/GeneAgent/GPT-Hu compared with error bars in panel f."]}, {"figure_index": 2, "page_no": 6, "caption": "Figure 2: BFT learns representations with biological meaning. a : UMAP visualization of gene embeddings. From left to right are the gene embeddings of scGPT, the text embeddings of gene descriptions output by OpenAI ChatGPT, and the text embeddings of gene descriptions output by BFT-based DeepSeek-R1-Distill 70B. b : Representation evaluation at the gene level, with the task type being single-gene input. The classifier takes a single gene embedding as input and predicts its biological attributes, such as long-range and short-range transcription factors, dosage-sensitive and dosage-insensitive transcription factors, bivalent and Lys4-only methylated genes, and bivalent and non-methylated genes. c : Representation evaluation at the gene level, with the task type being multi-gene input. The embeddings of two genes or two proteins are concatenated, and the classifier predicts their interaction type. d : Representation evaluation at the cell level. On single-cell data, cell embeddings are obtained by aggregating gene embeddings, and the evaluation includes phenotypes and cell types. e : Comparison of multimodal integration at the cell level, with the goal of integrating the two modalities of RNA and ADT. The three main columns (Bio conservation, Batch correction, and Aggregate score) respectively represent biological heterogeneity, modality mixing degree, and the overall metric. Each main column contains specific sub-metrics. For the first two columns, the color gradient from purple to green indicates scores from low to high. f : Comparison of single-cell perturbation response prediction results, with zero-shot prediction conducted on four perturbation datasets respectively.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nMulti-panel scientific figure comparing gene/cell embedding or foundation-model methods.\n\nVisible panels:\n\n- **Panel a**: Three UMAP/t-SNE-like 2D embedding plots labeled **scGPT**, **GenePT**, and **BFT**. Points are colored by gene biotype annotations. Legend includes biological source objects/classes such as **IG_V_gene**, **IG_V_pseudogene**, **TR_J_gene**, **TR_V_gene**, **antisense**, **lncRNA**, **miRNA**, **misc_RNA**, **polymorphic_pseudogene**, **processed_pseudogene**, **processed_transcript**, **protein_coding**, **pseudogene**, **rRNA**, **sense_intronic**, **snRNA**, **snoRNA**, **transcribed_unprocessed_pseudogene**, and **unprocessed_pseudogene**. The embeddings show different clustering/separation patterns across models.\n\n- **Panel b**: Bar chart titled **Single gene input**, y-axis **AUC**, comparing methods **scGPT**, **GenePT**, and **BFT** across tasks: **TF range**, **Dosage sensitivity**, **Bivalent vs non-bivalent**, and **Bivalent vs Utx**. BFT appears highest or near-highest for several tasks, with numeric AUC labels above bars.\n\n- **Panel c**: Bar chart titled **Multi gene input**, y-axis **AUC**, comparing **scGPT**, **GenePT**, and **BFT** on **GO** and **PPI** tasks. BFT has the highest visible scores in both categories.\n\n- **Panel d**: Two grouped bar charts for clustering metrics. Top: **Clustering performance (phenotype)** with metrics **ARI**, **AMI**, **ASW**. Bottom: **Clustering performance (cell type)** with the same metrics. Methods shown are **PCA**, **scGPT**, **GenePT**, and **BFT**. BFT shows the strongest visible clustering scores, especially for cell type AMI.\n\n- **Panel e**: Table-like benchmark summary comparing integration methods **scMODAL**, **BFT**, **Harmony**, and **BBKNN**. Columns are grouped into **Bio conservation**, **Batch correction**, and **Aggregate score**. Metrics include **Isolated labels**, **KMeans NMI**, **KMeans ARI**, **Silhouette label**, **CLISI**, **Silhouette batch**, **iLISI**, **KBET**, **Graph connectivity**, **PCR comparison**, plus aggregate **Batch correction**, **Bio conservation**, and **Total**. Colored circles and bars encode scores. **scMODAL** has the highest total score shown.\n\n- **Panel f**: Four small bar charts labeled **hepg2**, **rpe1**, **jurkat**, and **k562**. Y-axis is **Score**. Categories include **DES**, **PDS**, and **MAE**. Methods in the legend: **BFT**, **STATE-SE**, and **STATE-small**. Bars include error bars. The plots compare method performance across cell-line contexts.\n\nOverall finding shown visually: the figure benchmarks multiple models/methods, especially **BFT**, **scGPT**, **GenePT**, **scMODAL**, **Harmony**, **BBKNN**, and **STATE** variants, across gene embedding structure, gene-level prediction AUCs, clustering metrics, multimodal/batch integration scores, and perturbation or cell-line prediction scores."]}, {"figure_index": 3, "page_no": 13, "caption": "Extended Data Figure 1: Ablation study. a : Test results on different mathematical reasoning datasets. We set two baselines: the red dashed line represents SFT, and the blue dashed line represents reinforcement learning (represented by GRPO). BFT includes three window length settings (BFT-128, BFT-256, and BFT-512). BFT w/o sample denotes removing the sample-level weighting mechanism from BFT (this setting does not require a sliding window). BFT w/o token denotes removing the token-level weighting mechanism from BFT (this setting requires a sliding window). b : Tracking the reasoning performance of BFT (with different window length settings) within 1 training epoch.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel quantitative figure with panels labeled **a** and **b**.\n\nPanel **a** shows three bar charts for benchmark accuracy:\n- **math_oai**\n- **minerva_math**\n- **olympiadbench**\n\nEach chart has y-axis **Accuracy (%)** and x-axis methods including **SFT**, **GRPO**, **BFT-128**, **BFT-256**, **BFT-512**, **BFT w/o sample**, and token-ablation variants such as **BFT-128 w/o token**, **BFT-256 w/o token**, **BFT-512 w/o token**. Dashed horizontal reference lines indicate **GRPO baseline** in blue and **SFT baseline** in red.\n\nPanel **b** shows three line plots for the same benchmarks, plotting **Accuracy (%)** versus **Global Step**. Curves compare **BFT-128**, **BFT-256**, and **BFT-512**, with a legend below.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy images, anatomical structures, or biological transformations are visible. The figure appears to report machine learning or reasoning-model benchmark results. The visible finding is that BFT variants generally improve over SFT and approach or exceed GRPO baseline performance, with performance varying by benchmark, BFT size, token ablation, and training step."]}, {"figure_index": 4, "page_no": 14, "caption": "Extended Data Figure 2: The training runtime (unit: seconds) and evaluation scores of different methods. BFT includes three window length settings (128, 256, and 512), and the comparison methods include SFT and Focal loss. The training runtime of BFT is close to that of SFT, while its evaluation score is far higher than that of SFT.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a single-panel scatter/bubble plot comparing methods by `Runtime` on the x-axis and `Score` on the y-axis. Points are color-coded by `Method`, with a legend listing `SFT`, `Focal loss`, `BFT-256`, `BFT-128`, and `BFT-512`. Bubble size represents `Metrics`, with a separate size legend showing values approximately `0.016`, `0.038`, `0.093`, `0.106`, and `0.111`.\n\nVisible findings:\n- `BFT-256` has the highest score, labeled about `0.111`, at runtime around `9880`.\n- `BFT-512` is slightly lower, labeled about `0.106`, at runtime around `9820`.\n- `BFT-128` is labeled about `0.093`, at runtime around `9750`.\n- `SFT` is much lower, labeled about `0.038`, at runtime around `9670`.\n- `Focal loss` is lowest, labeled about `0.016`, at runtime around `11050`.\n\nNo biological source objects, microscopy/image transformations, or model interface diagrams are visible."]}, {"figure_index": 5, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel: single visible panel labeled **a**.\n\nDescription: The figure shows a workflow for converting gene information into embeddings. A **User prompt** box contains “Tell me about gene.” This input is passed through a **DeepSeek** model labeled **LLM-BFT**, producing an **LLM response** box with text such as “Gene name, function, interaction…”. The response is transformed into separate text entries labeled **Text of gene x** through **Text of gene y**, indicating multiple gene-specific textual descriptions. These texts are then passed into **Youtu-Embedding**, labeled **Text to embedding**, producing a **Gene embedding (N,2048)** matrix shown as a pink heatmap-like grid.\n\nBiological source objects: genes represented as gene-specific text descriptions.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces: user prompt → DeepSeek LLM response → gene text descriptions → Youtu-Embedding text-to-embedding model → numerical gene embedding matrix.\n\nFindings: The panel illustrates a pipeline for deriving 2048-dimensional gene embeddings from LLM-generated gene descriptions; no experimental results or quantitative findings are shown."]}, {"figure_index": 6, "page_no": 16, "caption": "", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nPanel `b` shows a schematic matrix transformation. On the left is a gene-expression-like matrix with rows labeled `Cell 1` through `Cell M` and columns labeled `Gene 1 ... Gene N`, shown as blue intensity blocks. This matrix is multiplied by a `Gene embedding (N,2048)` matrix shown in pink, with rows labeled `Gene 1 ... Gene N`. An arrow points to the resulting `Cell embedding (M,2048)` matrix on the right, shown as blue intensity blocks with rows labeled `Cell 1 ... Cell M`.\n\nVisible transformation: cell-by-gene input matrix × gene-embedding matrix produces a cell-embedding matrix. Biological source objects represented are cells and genes. Model interface/dimensionality shown: genes are embedded into 2048-dimensional vectors, yielding 2048-dimensional cell embeddings for `M` cells. No specific organism, dataset, model name, or empirical finding is visible in the image."]}, {"figure_index": 7, "page_no": 17, "caption": "Extended Data Figure 7: UMAP visualization of the multi-gene input task. a : For GGI, the input embedding of the classifier is directly concatenated from the embeddings of two genes. b : For PPI, the input embedding of the classifier is directly concatenated from the embeddings of two proteins.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with two labeled rows, `a` and `b`, comparing embedding visualizations from three models: `scGPT`, `GenePT`, and `BFT`.\n\nPanel `a` shows UMAP scatter plots titled `Gene Pairs Colored by Interaction Label` for `scGPT` and `GenePT`, and `Protein Pairs Colored by Interaction Label` for `BFT`. Points are colored by binary interaction label: blue `0` and orange `1`. Axes are labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. The plots show different spatial separations of positive and negative interaction pairs across the three model-derived embeddings.\n\nPanel `b` shows UMAP scatter plots titled `Protein Pairs Colored by Interaction Label` for `scGPT`, `GenePT`, and `BFT`. Points are colored by interaction category labels: `activation`, `binding`, `catalysis`, `expression`, `inhibition`, `ptmod`, and `reaction`. Axes are again labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`. The biological source objects are gene pairs and protein pairs, transformed into low-dimensional UMAP embeddings from model representations. The visible comparison suggests that the models organize interaction pairs differently, with BFT showing more structured separated clusters in panel `b` than the more compact or diffuse scGPT and GenePT protein-pair plots."]}, {"figure_index": 8, "page_no": 17, "caption": "Extended Data Figure 8: UMAP visualization of cell-level embeddings. a : PCA embeddings of the raw data, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively. b : Cell embeddings derived from LLM-BFT, colored by cell type labels (cell type heterogeneity), patient labels (batch labels), and phenotype labels (disease heterogeneity), respectively.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure with two rows labeled **a** and **b**. Each row contains three UMAP scatter plots with axes labeled **UMAP1** and **UMAP2**.\n\nPanel **a**:\n- Three UMAP embeddings are shown for the same dataset or analysis view.\n- Left plot is colored by **celltype**, with legend entries including BCell, EC, Fibroblast, MSC, Mastcell, MonoMphDC, NK, Plasma, SMC1, SMC2, Tcell, and Unknown.\n- Middle plot is colored by **patient**, with affected samples **affected_1** through **affected_8** and controls **control_1** through **control_3**.\n- Right plot is colored by **phenotype**, with categories **Ascending only**, **Ascending to descending**, **Ascending w/ root**, and **Control**.\n- The plotted biological source objects appear to be single cells or cell/nucleus profiles embedded in 2D UMAP space.\n\nPanel **b**:\n- Three additional UMAP scatter plots with the same labeling scheme: **celltype**, **patient**, and **phenotype**.\n- The UMAP structure differs from panel a, showing larger, more continuous clusters.\n- The same cell-type and patient/phenotype legends are visible.\n- This likely represents another subset, preprocessing state, or embedding resolution, but the specific transformation is not stated in the image.\n\nVisible transformations/model interfaces:\n- Dimensionality reduction/embedding using **UMAP**.\n- Color mapping by metadata annotations: cell type, patient identity, and phenotype group.\n\nVisible findings:\n- Distinct cell-type clusters are present in UMAP space.\n- Patient identities and phenotype groups are distributed across multiple clusters rather than confined to a single visible region.\n- Some phenotype categories show enriched regions within parts of the embeddings, especially in the rightmost plots of both panels."]}, {"figure_index": 9, "page_no": 18, "caption": "Extended Data Figure 9: UMAP visualization of single-cell multimodal data integration results. Rows 1 to 4 represent different integration methods, respectively. Columns 1 to 3 correspond to different coloring labels (modality, cell type, and donor), respectively.", "vlm_descriptions": ["SCIENTIFIC_FIGURE\n\nThe image is a multi-panel scientific figure showing UMAP embeddings for single-cell multimodal data integration/comparison.\n\nVisible structure:\n- 4 rows labeled: `BFT`, `BBKNN`, `Harmony`, `scMODAL`\n- 3 columns labeled: `modality`, `celltype`, `donor`\n- Each panel has axes labeled `UMAP1` and `UMAP2`\n\nPanel contents:\n- `modality` panels color points by assay/modality:\n  - `ADT` in blue\n  - `RNA` in orange\n- `celltype` panels color points by immune cell annotation:\n  - `B`\n  - `CD4 T`\n  - `CD8 T`\n  - `DC`\n  - `Mono`\n  - `NK`\n  - `other`\n  - `other T`\n- `donor` panels color points by donor/sample:\n  - `P1` through `P8`\n\nBiological source objects:\n- Single-cell measurements from immune cell populations.\n- Modalities shown are RNA and ADT, suggesting paired or integrated single-cell transcriptomic and antibody-derived tag data.\n- Donor labels indicate multiple biological samples or individuals.\n\nTransformations/model interfaces:\n- All panels show dimensionality reduction via UMAP.\n- Rows compare different batch correction or integration methods: BFT, BBKNN, Harmony, and scMODAL.\n- The same embeddings are visualized under different metadata colorings: modality, cell type, and donor.\n\nVisible findings:\n- `BFT` shows substantial overlap between ADT and RNA in the modality panel, with cell-type structure visible and donor colors mixed.\n- `BBKNN` shows stronger separation or structure by modality, with RNA and ADT occupying partially distinct regions.\n- `Harmony` shows several separated clusters with modality mixing varying across regions.\n- `scMODAL` shows broad overlap between ADT and RNA across major clusters, with clearer cell-type grouping and mixed donor colors."]}]

