ts in the Mercer et al dataset. (B) The UMAP visualization of branchpoint and non-branchpoint site embeddings (only adenine sites are shown). (C) The precision-recall curves of the models for predicting branchpoints. (D) The impact of variants at different sites on SpliceBERT predicted BP scores and (E) the corresponding logos of wild-type sequences. (F) The predicted BP score change induced by different types of mutations at BP sites and their 2nt- and 3nt- upstream.\n\n<!-- image -->",
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_12/full_2026-07-06__rec_001784/screening_evidence_summary.json:893:        "text": "Figure 1\n\nFigure 1. An overview of SpliceBERT. (A) SpliceBERT was pre-trained on the pre-mRNA sequences of 72 vertebrates under the masked language  modeling  (MLM)  framework.  We  additionally  applied  SpliceBERT  for  downstream  tasks  of  (a)  unsupervised  variant  effect prediction, (b) visualization of nucleotide embeddings, (c) cross-species splice sites prediction and (d) branchpoints prediction and variant analysis. (B) The accuracy of masked language modelling (MLM ACC) of SpliceBERT and the fraction of repeats in different genomic function regions. (C) The distribution of phastCons(100way) score of randomly selected sites in pre-mRNA sequences. (D) The precisionrecall curves of linear regression (LR) models for predicting conservative/non-conservative sites based on SpliceBERT, SpliceBERT-human and one-hot encoded nucleotide embeddings.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 2\n\nFigure  2. SpliceBERT  enables  unsupervised  splice-disrupting  variant  prediction.    The  relationship  between  the  median  value  of  KLdivergence between the MLM logits of wild-type sequences and mutant sequences and the distances to variants in (A) conservative and nonconservative  sites  sampled  by  in  silico  mutagenesis,  (B)  VexSeq  SDV  (splice-disrupting  variant)  and  non-SDV  (non-splice-disrupting variant)  group, and (C) MFASS SDV and non-SDV group. The boxplot of delta-logit ( logit), the sum of KL-divergence in neighboring nucleotides  (KL-div(context))  and  the  KL-divergence  at  splice  donors/acceptors  (KL-div(SS))  of  the  SDV  and  non-SDV  group  in  (D) VexSeq and (E) MFASS. The precision-recall curves of SpliceBERT and baseline methods for classifying SDV and non-SDV samples in (F) VexSeq and (G) MFASS.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 3\n\nFigure  3. UMAP  visualization  of  splice  site  embeddings.  Visualization  of  canonical  splice  sites  and  non-splice  sites  with  GT  and  AG dinucleotides based on (A) SpliceBERT embeddings, (B) DNABERT(k=3) embeddings and (C) one-hot encodings.  Visualization of splice sites  of  high  and  low  usage  in  K562  cell  line  estimated  by  SpliSER  based  on  (D)  SpliceBERT  embeddings,  (E)  DNABERT(k=3) embeddings or (F) one-hot encodings.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 4\n\nFigure 4. Analysis of the attention weights in SpliceBERT. (A) The distribution of attention weights between donors and acceptors from the same intron (D-A(intron)), the same exon (D-A(exon)), different intron/exon (D-A(unpair)), between donors and donors (D-D) and between acceptors and acceptors (A-A). A control group of randomly paired nuelcotides is also shown (random pair). The average attention weights across  all  the  6  layers  in  SpliceBERT  were  used.  The  statistical  significance  was  assessed  with  t-test. (B) The  distribution  of phastCons(100way) scores around donors and acceptors. The average value and standard error of the conservations scores in 1000 randomly selected donor-acceptor pairs (from the same introns) are shown. (C) The distribution of donors' and acceptor's atteiton around acceptors and  donors,  respectively,  in  each  Transformer  layer.  The  median  attention  weights  across  different  samples  were  used.  (D-A:  donoracceptor, D-D: donor-donor, A-A: acceptor-acceptor, L: layer. ***: P-value &lt; )\n\nFigure 5. SpliceBERT predicts splice sites in different species. (A) The F1-score of SpliceBERT and baseline models on 5 different species. The visualization of the impact of in silico mutagenesis variants on splice site scores for (B) donors and (D) acceptors and corresponding sequence logos of wild-type sequences (C and E).\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 6\n\nFigure 6. SpliceBERT predicts human branchpoint sites. (A) The distribution of high-confidence branchpoints in the Mercer et al dataset. (B) The UMAP visualization of branchpoint and non-branchpoint site embeddings (only adenine sites are shown). (C) The precision-recall curves of the models for predicting branchpoints. (D) The impact of variants at different sites on SpliceBERT predicted BP scores and (E) the corresponding logos of wild-type sequences. (F) The predicted BP score change induced by different types of mutations at BP sites and their 2nt- and 3nt- upstream.\n\n<!-- image -->",
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_12/full_2026-07-06__rec_001784/screening_evidence_summary.json:1071:        "text": "Self-supervised learning on millions of pre-mRNA sequences improves sequence-based RNA splicing prediction\nTABLE AND FIGURES LEGENDS\nFigure 1\nFigure 1. An overview of SpliceBERT. (A) SpliceBERT was pre-trained on the pre-mRNA sequences of 72 vertebrates under the masked language  modeling  (MLM)  framework.  We  additionally  applied  SpliceBERT  for  downstream  tasks  of  (a)  unsupervised  variant  effect prediction, (b) visualization of nucleotide embeddings, (c) cross-species splice sites prediction and (d) branchpoints prediction and variant analysis. (B) The accuracy of masked language modelling (MLM ACC) of SpliceBERT and the fraction of repeats in different genomic function regions. (C) The distribution of phastCons(100way) score of randomly selected sites in pre-mRNA sequences. (D) The precisionrecall curves of linear regression (LR) models for predicting conservative/non-conservative sites based on SpliceBERT, SpliceBERT-human and one-hot encoded nucleotide embeddings.\nFigure 2\nFigure  2. SpliceBERT  enables  unsupervised  splice-disrupting  variant  prediction.    The  relationship  between  the  median  value  of  KLdivergence between the MLM logits of wild-type sequences and mutant sequences and the distances to variants in (A) conservative and nonconservative  sites  sampled  by  in  silico  mutagenesis,  (B)  VexSeq  SDV  (splice-disrupting  variant)  and  non-SDV  (non-splice-disrupting variant)  group, and (C) MFASS SDV and non-SDV group. The boxplot of delta-logit ( logit), the sum of KL-divergence in neighboring nucleotides  (KL-div(context))  and  the  KL-divergence  at  splice  donors/acceptors  (KL-div(SS))  of  the  SDV  and  non-SDV  group  in  (D) VexSeq and (E) MFASS. The precision-recall curves of SpliceBERT and baseline methods for classifying SDV and non-SDV samples in (F) VexSeq and (G) MFASS.\nFigure 3\nFigure  3. UMAP  visualization  of  splice  site  embeddings.  Visualization  of  canonical  splice  sites  and  non-splice  sites  with  GT  and  AG dinucleotides based on (A) SpliceBERT embeddings, (B) DNABERT(k=3) embeddings and (C) one-hot encodings.  Visualization of splice sites  of  high  and  low  usage  in  K562  cell  line  estimated  by  SpliSER  based  on  (D)  SpliceBERT  embeddings,  (E)  DNABERT(k=3) embeddings or (F) one-hot encodings.\nFigure 4",
./data/docling_graph_screening_evidence_full_2026-07-10_gpt54mini_direct_nolimits_16shards/shard_12/full_2026-07-06__rec_001784/screening_evidence_summary.json:1130:          "text": "Figure 1\n\nFigure 1. An overview of SpliceBERT. (A) SpliceBERT was pre-trained on the pre-mRNA sequences of 72 vertebrates under the masked language  modeling  (MLM)  framework.  We  additionally  applied  SpliceBERT  for  downstream  tasks  of  (a)  unsupervised  variant  effect prediction, (b) visualization of nucleotide embeddings, (c) cross-species splice sites prediction and (d) branchpoints prediction and variant analysis. (B) The accuracy of masked language modelling (MLM ACC) of SpliceBERT and the fraction of repeats in different genomic function regions. (C) The distribution of phastCons(100way) score of randomly selected sites in pre-mRNA sequences. (D) The precisionrecall curves of linear regression (LR) models for predicting conservative/non-conservative sites based on SpliceBERT, SpliceBERT-human and one-hot encoded nucleotide embeddings.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 2\n\nFigure  2. SpliceBERT  enables  unsupervised  splice-disrupting  variant  prediction.    The  relationship  between  the  median  value  of  KLdivergence between the MLM logits of wild-type sequences and mutant sequences and the distances to variants in (A) conservative and nonconservative  sites  sampled  by  in  silico  mutagenesis,  (B)  VexSeq  SDV  (splice-disrupting  variant)  and  non-SDV  (non-splice-disrupting variant)  group, and (C) MFASS SDV and non-SDV group. The boxplot of delta-logit ( logit), the sum of KL-divergence in neighboring nucleotides  (KL-div(context))  and  the  KL-divergence  at  splice  donors/acceptors  (KL-div(SS))  of  the  SDV  and  non-SDV  group  in  (D) VexSeq and (E) MFASS. The precision-recall curves of SpliceBERT and baseline methods for classifying SDV and non-SDV samples in (F) VexSeq and (G) MFASS.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 3\n\nFigure  3. UMAP  visualization  of  splice  site  embeddings.  Visualization  of  canonical  splice  sites  and  non-splice  sites  with  GT  and  AG dinucleotides based on (A) SpliceBERT embeddings, (B) DNABERT(k=3) embeddings and (C) one-hot encodings.  Visualization of splice sites  of  high  and  low  usage  in  K562  cell  line  estimated  by  SpliSER  based  on  (D)  SpliceBERT  embeddings,  (E)  DNABERT(k=3) embeddings or (F) one-hot encodings.\n\n<!-- image -->\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 4\n\nFigure 4. Analysis of the attention weights in SpliceBERT. (A) The distribution of attention weights between donors and acceptors from the same intron (D-A(intron)), the same exon (D-A(exon)), different intron/exon (D-A(unpair)), between donors and donors (D-D) and between acceptors and acceptors (A-A). A control group of randomly paired nuelcotides is also shown (random pair). The average attention weights across  all  the  6  layers  in  SpliceBERT  were  used.  The  statistical  significance  was  assessed  with  t-test. (B) The  distribution  of phastCons(100way) scores around donors and acceptors. The average value and standard error of the conservations scores in 1000 randomly selected donor-acceptor pairs (from the same introns) are shown. (C) The distribution of donors' and acceptor's atteiton around acceptors and  donors,  respectively,  in  each  Transformer  layer.  The  median  attention  weights  across  different  samples  were  used.  (D-A:  donoracceptor, D-D: donor-donor, A-A: acceptor-acceptor, L: layer. ***: P-value &lt; )\n\nFigure 5. SpliceBERT predicts splice sites in different species. (A) The F1-score of SpliceBERT and baseline models on 5 different species. The visualization of the impact of in silico mutagenesis variants on splice site scores for (B) donors and (D) acceptors and corresponding sequence logos of wild-type sequences (C and E).\n\n<!-- image -->\n\nFigure 6\n\nFigure 6. SpliceBERT predicts human branchpoint sites. (A) The distribution of high-confidence branchpoints in the Mercer et al dataset. (B) The UMAP visualization of branchpoint and non-branchpoint site embeddings (only adenine sites are shown). (C) The precision-recall curves of the models for predicting branchpoints. (D) The impact of variants at different sites on SpliceBERT predicted BP scores and (E) the corresponding logos of wild-type sequences. (F) The predicted BP score change induced by different types of mutations at BP sites and their 2nt- and 3nt- upstream.\n\n<!-- image -->",
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/rec_003349_repozitorij.md:189:This thesis addresses this fundamental gap by investigating whether foundation models can transcend their training domains to provide meaningful insights into organisms far removed from their original scope. We evaluate these models on the social amoeba Dictyostelium discoideum , chosen specifically because it represents an ideal test case for cross-species generalization. As shown in Table 1.1, this organism is separated from typical training species by hundreds of millions of years of evolution [7], with only about 30% of its genes orthologous to human genes [14]. This evolutionary relationship is further illustrated in the phylogenetic context (Figure 1.1). Despite this evolutionary distance, D. discoideum possesses well-characterized developmental biology with clear temporal dynamics, measurable cell fate trajectories, and established molecular markers that provide rigorous biological ground truth for evaluation [8].
./data/docling_uncertain_problem_retry_no_vlm_2026-07-08/markdown/rec_003349_repozitorij.md:211:Figure 1.1: Evolutionary divergence times between Dictyostelium discoideum and foundation model training species. The social amoeba is separated from commonly used training organisms (shown in bold) by hundreds of millions of years of evolution [7].
